{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And one lot for Azazel</b> — a high mountain, a hard cliff, as it is said (Leviticus 16:22) \"a rugged land\" (אֶרֶץ גְּזֵרָה); this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 16:8). And in Torat Kohanim (chapter 2:8): \"For Azazel\" — to the hardest place among the mountains. One might think [this means] in a settled area — Scripture therefore says \"to the wilderness\" (Leviticus 16:21). And from where do we know it must be a cliff (ibid. v. 22)? Scripture therefore says \"to a rugged land.\" According to this, the explanation of the word \"לַעֲזָאזֵל\" would be: \"to the strong/hard,\" and it is a doubled zayin, like \"strong and mighty\" (עִזּוּז וְגִבּוֹר, Psalms 24:8). \n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham Ibn Ezra wrote: Rabbi Samuel said — even though it is written regarding the sin-offering goat that it is for HaShem, the sent-away goat too is for HaShem. But there is no need for this, for the sent-away goat is not an offering at all, since it is not slaughtered. And if you can grasp the secret that is behind the word עֲזָאזֵל, you will know its secret and the secret of its name, for it has companions in Scripture. And I shall reveal to you part of the secret by hint: when you reach thirty-three (i.e., after 33 verses — see Leviticus 17:7) you will know it. And behold, Ibn Ezra is a faithful spirit who conceals the matter, whereas I, the discloser, reveal his secret — for our Rabbis, of blessed memory, have already revealed it in many places. \n\nThey said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 65:10): \"And the goat shall bear upon him\" (Leviticus 16:22) — this is Esau, as it is said (Genesis 27:11) \"Behold, Esau my brother is a hairy man\" (אִישׁ שָׂעִיר). \"All their iniquities\" — the iniquities of the perfect one (תָּם), as it is said \"and Jacob was a perfect man\" (אִישׁ תָּם, Genesis 25:27). And it is spelled out from this in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer ha-Gadol (chapter 46): therefore they would give a bribe to Samael on Yom ha-Kippurim so that he would not nullify their sacrifice, as it is said \"one lot for YHWH and one lot for Azazel\" — the lot of the Holy One, blessed be He, for an elevation-offering, and the lot of Azazel a sin-offering goat, and all the iniquities of Israel upon him, as it is said \"and the goat shall bear upon him.\" Samael saw that no sin was found among them on Yom ha-Kippurim; he said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Master of all worlds, You have one nation on earth like the ministering angels in heaven. Just as the ministering angels are barefoot, so Israel are barefoot on Yom ha-Kippurim; just as the ministering angels have neither eating nor drinking, so Israel have neither eating nor drinking on Yom ha-Kippurim; just as the ministering angels do not sit, so Israel stand on their feet on Yom ha-Kippurim; just as the ministering angels make peace among themselves, so Israel make peace among themselves on Yom ha-Kippurim; just as the ministering angels are clean of all sin, so Israel are clean of all sin on Yom ha-Kippurim. And the Holy One, blessed be He, hears the testimony of Israel from their accuser and makes atonement for the altar and for the sanctuary and for the priests and for all the congregation of the people, as it is said (Leviticus 16:33) \"and he shall make atonement for the holy sanctuary\" etc. — thus far this aggadah. \n\nNow behold, they have informed us of his name and his deed, and this is the secret of the matter: they would worship other elohim — these are the angels — making offerings to them, and they were to them for a pleasing fragrance, as it is said (Ezekiel 16:18–19) \"And you set My oil and My incense before them; and My bread that I gave you, fine flour and oil and honey, with which I fed you — you set it before them for a pleasing fragrance, says Adonai YHWH.\" And you must reflect on this from Scripture and from the Masoretic tradition. \n\nNow the Torah forbade entirely accepting their divinity and any worship of them, but the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded that on Yom ha-Kippurim a goat be sent into the wilderness to the prince who rules over places of desolation — for this is what is fitting for him, since he is their master, and from the emanation of his power come desolation and waste. For he is the cause of the stars of the sword and of blood and of wars and of strife and of wounds and of blows and of separation and of destruction; and in general, the soul in the sphere of Mars, and his portion among the nations is Esau, who is the nation that inherits the sword and wars, and among the beasts, the hairy he-goats and the goats; and in his portion too are the demons called מַזִּיקִין in the language of our Rabbis, and in the language of Scripture (Leviticus 17:7) \"שְׂעִירִים\" — for so he and his nation are called \"שֵׂעִיר\" (hairy goat). \n\nAnd the intention in the sent-away goat is not that it be an offering from us to him, heaven forbid, but rather that our intention be to do the will of our Creator who thus commanded us. And the parable for this is like one who prepared a feast for a master, and the master commanded the one preparing the feast: \"Give one portion to so-and-so, my servant.\" The one preparing the feast gives nothing at all to that servant, nor does he do it for his honor; rather, everything is given to the master, and the master gives a portion to his servant — and this one keeps his commandment and does for the honor of the master all that he commanded him. And indeed, the master, out of compassion for the one who prepared the feast, wanted all his servants to enjoy from it, so that they would speak of his praise and not his disgrace. \n\nThis is the reason for the lots: for had the priest designated them verbally, saying \"for YHWH\" and \"for Azazel,\" it would have been as though he were worshipping him and vowing in his name. But he would place them before YHWH at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting (Leviticus 16:7), for both of them are a gift to YHWH, and He gave from them to His servant the portion that comes to him from HaShem — He cast the lot for them, and His hand apportioned them, as it is said (Proverbs 16:33) \"The lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from YHWH.\" And even after the lot, he would stand it before YHWH, to declare that it is His and that we have no intention in sending it except as a will-offering to HaShem, as it is said (Leviticus 16:10) \"it shall be set alive before YHWH, to make atonement upon it, to send it away\" etc. — and therefore we do not slaughter it at all. \n\nAnd Onkelos translated (Onkelos on Leviticus 16:8) (here) \"לִשְׁמָא דַה'\" — \"for the name of YHWH\" — and \"for Azazel\": for the one is for the Name of YHWH and not for Him [as an independent recipient], and the second is for Azazel and not for the name of Azazel. And because of this, our Rabbis said (Sifra, Tazria, chapter 13:9) regarding \"and My statutes\" (וְאֶת חֻקֹּתַי, Leviticus 18:4) — these are matters against which the evil inclination objects and the nations of the world find fault: the wearing of sha'atnez, the red heifer, and the sent-away goat. And they found no rebuttal to the nations of the world regarding the [other] offerings, for they are on the fires of YHWH; but regarding the sent-away goat they will object, for they will think that we do as their deeds. And so too with the red heifer, because it is performed outside the camp and its matter resembles the matter of the sent-away goat — to remove the spirit of impurity, as it is said concerning the future (Zechariah 13:2) \"I will remove the prophets and the spirit of impurity from the land.\" \n\nFrom this you will understand the reason for washing the garments of the one who sends the goat to Azazel and the one who burns the heifer, and what our Rabbis mentioned (Zevachim 94a) regarding the washing of the garments of the bulls that are burned and the goats that are burned. \n\nBehold, Ibn Ezra hinted to you that you will know its secret when you reach the verse \"And they shall no longer sacrifice their sacrifices to the goat-demons\" (Leviticus 17:7) — and the word is composite, and its companions are many. And behold, the matter is clear — except if you investigate what relevance the separate intelligences and the spirits have to an offering; and this will become known regarding spirits through the wisdom of negromancy, and will also become known regarding the intelligibles through the hints of the Torah for one who understands their secret. And I cannot explain, for we would need to muzzle the mouths of those who are arrogant about nature, who follow the Greek [Aristotle] who denied everything except what is perceptible to him, and who presumed to think — he and his wicked disciples — that every matter his reasoning could not reach is not truth."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra's cryptic hint 'when you reach thirty-three' is glossed parenthetically in the Hebrew as 'after 33 verses — Leviticus 17:7'; I retained the parenthetical formula and also inserted the verse reference for clarity, as Ramban's own text includes a parenthetical citation at that point. The wisdom of 'נגרומנסי\"א' refers to necromancy (נגרומנסיא / nigromancia) and is left transliterated as Ramban wrote it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:02.559232567Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}